WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.644 --> 00:00:01.477
- Hi everyone.

00:00:01.477 --> 00:00:03.374
Sal Khan here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.374 --> 00:00:05.884
Welcome to our homeroom live stream.

00:00:05.884 --> 00:00:09.174
We have a very exciting
guest, Rehema Ellis,

00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:12.954
who is the education correspondent
for the NBC Nightly News.

00:00:12.954 --> 00:00:14.444
But before we get into that

00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:16.664
what promises to be very
exciting conversation,

00:00:16.664 --> 00:00:18.254
I'll give my standard announcements.

00:00:18.254 --> 00:00:20.974
One, a reminder that
we are a not-for-profit

00:00:20.974 --> 00:00:23.234
and we can only exist through
philanthropic donations

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:24.744
from folks like yourself.

00:00:24.744 --> 00:00:26.024
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:26.024 --> 00:00:30.094
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:30.094 --> 00:00:32.144
Also want to make a shout out

00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:34.444
to several organizations
that have stepped up

00:00:34.444 --> 00:00:35.824
to help Khan Academy,

00:00:35.824 --> 00:00:39.214
especially during the
COVID school closure,

00:00:39.214 --> 00:00:41.314
what can only be described as a crisis.

00:00:41.314 --> 00:00:43.854
Where Khan Academy's traffic
has gone up dramatically,

00:00:43.854 --> 00:00:45.244
our costs have gone up.

00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:48.434
So special thanks to Bank Of
America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:51.134
Fastly and Novartis to
help close that gap.

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:52.244
But we also need more,

00:00:52.244 --> 00:00:54.554
especially as we go into 2021.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:59.364
Last reminder, there is a
version of this live stream

00:00:59.364 --> 00:01:00.994
in podcast form.

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:03.364
Homeroom with Sal, The podcast.

00:01:03.364 --> 00:01:06.104
Get that wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:06.104 --> 00:01:10.364
So with that, I'm excited
to welcome Rehema Ellis

00:01:10.364 --> 00:01:12.674
to our conversation today.

00:01:12.674 --> 00:01:14.654
Rehema. Good to see you.

00:01:14.654 --> 00:01:15.804
- Nice to see you, Sal.

00:01:17.584 --> 00:01:19.711
- There's so much I want
to talk to you about.

00:01:19.711 --> 00:01:21.634
But the two things I think
will be really interesting

00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:24.454
and I encourage everyone
listening or seeing this

00:01:24.454 --> 00:01:26.914
on YouTube and Facebook to ask questions,

00:01:26.914 --> 00:01:29.088
especially in these two
dimensions, but really on anything.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.884
You're the education correspondent.

00:01:31.884 --> 00:01:34.384
So you're really seeing on the ground

00:01:34.384 --> 00:01:35.314
what's happening in schools.

00:01:35.314 --> 00:01:37.364
So I encourage people to
ask questions about that.

00:01:37.364 --> 00:01:39.685
But also just your life journey

00:01:39.685 --> 00:01:43.314
as a very successful journalist.

00:01:43.314 --> 00:01:44.877
Any questions people
have on that, as well.

00:01:44.877 --> 00:01:47.864
But maybe a good place to
start is just where are we?

00:01:47.864 --> 00:01:50.364
You know, what are you seeing
I know NBC Nightly News

00:01:50.364 --> 00:01:51.884
is going to be doing a whole series

00:01:51.884 --> 00:01:56.884
on understanding how teachers
are adapting to COVID.

00:01:56.924 --> 00:02:00.188
What has been some of your
biggest takeaways so far?

00:02:00.188 --> 00:02:04.004
- One of the things that is
my big takeaway is I hope

00:02:04.004 --> 00:02:06.634
that people will gain
a greater appreciation,

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:09.294
respect and recognition,
if you will, of teachers.

00:02:09.294 --> 00:02:11.344
As all parents, and I'm a parent myself,

00:02:11.344 --> 00:02:13.024
find ourselves at home with our children,

00:02:13.024 --> 00:02:16.334
we love them dearly and
care for them dearly,

00:02:16.334 --> 00:02:18.624
but some of us are not the best teachers

00:02:18.624 --> 00:02:20.254
for the ones that we love.

00:02:20.254 --> 00:02:23.754
And we are seeing a tremendous
amount of appreciation

00:02:23.754 --> 00:02:25.574
for what teachers do.

00:02:25.574 --> 00:02:27.164
We care for our children
because we love them,

00:02:27.164 --> 00:02:29.964
but we entrust them to
a stranger to care for

00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:33.404
some 25, sometimes 30
children in a classroom.

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:36.474
And for those who do it
with such magnificence

00:02:36.474 --> 00:02:38.664
and such passion and concern,

00:02:38.664 --> 00:02:42.574
they are really superstars in
terms of our children's lives.

00:02:42.574 --> 00:02:46.444
And ultimately in terms of
the life of our society,

00:02:46.444 --> 00:02:50.994
because a society to prosper
requires, demands really,

00:02:50.994 --> 00:02:52.494
an educated population.

00:02:52.494 --> 00:02:54.844
So, that's one of the
things that I'm seeing.

00:02:54.844 --> 00:02:56.331
I'm hearing a lot of people talking about,

00:02:56.331 --> 00:03:01.331
"Geez I had no idea how hard
it is to be an educator."

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:02.481
- Absolutely.

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:03.924
It's funny when you were saying,

00:03:03.924 --> 00:03:05.394
when you kind of reminded yourself,

00:03:05.394 --> 00:03:06.911
I love my children dearly.

00:03:06.911 --> 00:03:10.761
You expressed what a
lot of parents express,

00:03:10.761 --> 00:03:12.464
but especially during COVID,

00:03:12.464 --> 00:03:15.804
where we remind ourselves that
we love our children dearly.

00:03:15.804 --> 00:03:18.925
But maybe my children are a
little bit harder to deal with

00:03:18.925 --> 00:03:23.025
on a full day basis than
I originally expected.

00:03:23.025 --> 00:03:26.862
- For an untrained parent
who was not trained

00:03:26.862 --> 00:03:31.862
as an educator, you start to
see this is really challenging.

00:03:32.484 --> 00:03:35.044
And so this appreciation,
I think is wonderful.

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:36.814
And I'm so happy to be a part of

00:03:36.814 --> 00:03:38.494
what NBC Nightly News is doing.

00:03:38.494 --> 00:03:40.864
And that is devoting a
whole week to what we call

00:03:40.864 --> 00:03:43.584
Teacher Appreciation, to
really highlighting some

00:03:43.584 --> 00:03:45.924
of these superstars in
the field of education.

00:03:45.924 --> 00:03:47.407
And there are a lot of them.

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:51.350
- And it might be obvious, but you know

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:55.004
to NBC's credit, NBC has always kind of

00:03:55.004 --> 00:03:57.874
put a real emphasis on education.

00:03:57.874 --> 00:04:01.024
What was the catalyst
for doing this focus?

00:04:01.024 --> 00:04:03.484
A whole week on, not just
teacher appreciation,

00:04:03.484 --> 00:04:06.124
but how teachers are
navigating the crisis?

00:04:06.124 --> 00:04:08.304
- Well, one of the things is
the crisis that we are in.

00:04:08.304 --> 00:04:11.274
It's not just something that's
outside of the classrooms.

00:04:11.274 --> 00:04:13.234
Even our children are
not in the classrooms.

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:15.794
It's not just something that's
not happening to children.

00:04:15.794 --> 00:04:17.691
It is happening to children, even though

00:04:17.691 --> 00:04:21.606
children are not among
those whose numbers are seen

00:04:21.606 --> 00:04:25.954
as the fatalities of this dreaded virus.

00:04:25.954 --> 00:04:29.144
But children are being
severely impacted by it.

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:32.344
Not being in school, a place
that's a social environment.

00:04:32.344 --> 00:04:33.574
It's a learning environment.

00:04:33.574 --> 00:04:37.979
It's an opportunity to
learn how to not just

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:42.924
learn your ABCs, but how to
communicate in a community.

00:04:42.924 --> 00:04:45.934
When we don't have that,
that's really damaging.

00:04:45.934 --> 00:04:48.414
It's really impactful on children.

00:04:48.414 --> 00:04:51.804
NBC recognizes that and said,

00:04:51.804 --> 00:04:54.731
let's take a minute to see
what teachers are doing

00:04:54.731 --> 00:04:57.231
to try and fill that very important

00:04:57.231 --> 00:05:00.028
and large gap that's occurring
for so many of our children

00:05:00.028 --> 00:05:01.887
as they're learning remotely.

00:05:03.454 --> 00:05:05.494
- And, you know, I want to get into

00:05:05.494 --> 00:05:06.994
some of what y'all will cover

00:05:06.994 --> 00:05:08.314
but there's a question from YouTube.

00:05:08.314 --> 00:05:11.604
Mr. Specter asks, "Ms. Ellis,
is it hard finding stories?"

00:05:11.604 --> 00:05:12.634
And, that's actually interesting

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:13.704
because this is kind of the intersection

00:05:13.704 --> 00:05:15.224
of the two things I want to talk about.

00:05:15.224 --> 00:05:16.990
When y'all decide that you want to do

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.264
let's say a week of reporting on teaching,

00:05:19.264 --> 00:05:20.524
especially the crisis,

00:05:20.524 --> 00:05:21.544
what's the process for y'all?

00:05:21.544 --> 00:05:23.424
How do y'all find out
the interesting stories

00:05:23.424 --> 00:05:28.250
and how do you kind of surface
the real human element of it?

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.340
- It is varied.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.704
Sometimes it's complicated
and sometimes it's easy.

00:05:32.704 --> 00:05:35.304
Sometimes we'll see what
our colleagues have done

00:05:35.304 --> 00:05:37.394
on other platforms, be it newspaper

00:05:37.394 --> 00:05:39.634
other television programs, et cetera.

00:05:39.634 --> 00:05:41.934
And we'll say, "Oh my gosh,
that's a great story."

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:45.674
If we're looking from
local television programs,

00:05:45.674 --> 00:05:48.214
we'll have a producer, a camera person,

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:51.511
a correspondent like myself
in a particular city, and say

00:05:51.511 --> 00:05:53.563
"I saw this story on local television.

00:05:53.563 --> 00:05:55.164
This would be the kind of story

00:05:55.164 --> 00:05:57.873
that we should tell our
viewers all around the country

00:05:57.873 --> 00:06:00.074
and indeed all around the world."

00:06:00.074 --> 00:06:03.694
And then we sometimes
just call up schools.

00:06:03.694 --> 00:06:05.664
Call up school departments.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:09.881
Call up local community
agencies and ask them,

00:06:09.881 --> 00:06:12.704
"What's going on in your community
that might be of interest

00:06:12.704 --> 00:06:14.884
to our viewers from coast to coast?"

00:06:14.884 --> 00:06:17.734
So again, it's complicated sometimes.

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:20.224
And the process varies in terms of how we

00:06:20.224 --> 00:06:22.157
zero in on stories.

00:06:23.584 --> 00:06:25.814
- And what are some of
the stories, you know,

00:06:25.814 --> 00:06:27.644
not to give away your
whole week of reporting,

00:06:27.644 --> 00:06:28.974
but I'm assuming you've already started

00:06:28.974 --> 00:06:30.194
to record some of them.

00:06:30.194 --> 00:06:32.884
Are there any that have
really stood out to you?

00:06:32.884 --> 00:06:34.504
- Well, of course mine stands out to me

00:06:34.504 --> 00:06:36.314
but they all stand out to me.

00:06:36.314 --> 00:06:38.374
We had one on the air just last night

00:06:38.374 --> 00:06:40.304
about a teacher in Wisconsin.

00:06:40.304 --> 00:06:42.424
Who was so concerned about the fact that

00:06:42.424 --> 00:06:43.864
she wasn't able to be with her children

00:06:43.864 --> 00:06:45.484
because everything was shut down.

00:06:45.484 --> 00:06:46.654
It was being done remotely.

00:06:46.654 --> 00:06:49.534
And so she decided that she
was going to infuse some

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:52.043
of the sense of architectural
skills that she didn't have.

00:06:52.043 --> 00:06:54.001
At least she didn't know she had,

00:06:54.001 --> 00:06:56.424
but she found out that she did.

00:06:56.424 --> 00:06:59.704
And on her classroom,
on her school grounds,

00:06:59.704 --> 00:07:02.681
she built a tent with a tarp,

00:07:02.681 --> 00:07:07.681
the seats, the desk, the
chairs, the chalkboard,

00:07:08.213 --> 00:07:12.884
books, and brought her
classroom from home.

00:07:12.884 --> 00:07:14.054
Everybody was at home.

00:07:14.054 --> 00:07:17.234
Now they're in this classroom outside.

00:07:17.234 --> 00:07:19.054
It was so successful.

00:07:19.054 --> 00:07:22.614
The whole school now has
their classrooms outside.

00:07:22.614 --> 00:07:25.684
So, there's ventilation.
There is separation.

00:07:25.684 --> 00:07:27.774
Because they have a big property.

00:07:27.774 --> 00:07:30.214
That kind of thing doesn't
work so much in the inner city

00:07:30.214 --> 00:07:34.184
but it works wonderfully for
this school in Wisconsin.

00:07:34.184 --> 00:07:36.301
And kudos for that teacher for saying,

00:07:36.301 --> 00:07:39.184
"I'm gonna try something that
I never thought of before."

00:07:39.184 --> 00:07:41.144
That's exactly what's
happening with the story

00:07:41.144 --> 00:07:42.924
that I'm gonna have on tomorrow.

00:07:42.924 --> 00:07:45.524
It's about a science
teacher in Washington, DC.

00:07:45.524 --> 00:07:48.044
His name is Jonte Lee.

00:07:48.044 --> 00:07:50.244
And you can see from some of the pictures.

00:07:50.244 --> 00:07:54.411
He's concocted this
arrangement inside his own home

00:07:54.411 --> 00:07:56.391
because he said to himself,

00:07:56.391 --> 00:07:58.960
"We got a problem here
in need of a solution."

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:00.564
And as a science teacher,

00:08:00.564 --> 00:08:02.434
he said, "What can I do?"

00:08:02.434 --> 00:08:05.394
Listen to a little clip of our story.

00:08:05.394 --> 00:08:06.654
Chemistry and physics is hard to do

00:08:06.654 --> 00:08:07.934
when you're face to face.

00:08:07.934 --> 00:08:11.107
How did you imagine you would
continue teaching remotely?

00:08:11.974 --> 00:08:14.704
- Well, as you could see
that I turned my kitchen

00:08:14.704 --> 00:08:19.704
into my classroom and that my
kitchen also became my lab.

00:08:20.274 --> 00:08:22.554
The only way that I was able to think of

00:08:22.554 --> 00:08:24.444
doing it successfully was to make it as

00:08:24.444 --> 00:08:26.264
interactive as possible.

00:08:26.264 --> 00:08:28.774
And what better way to
teach chemistry then

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:29.607
from the kitchen?

00:08:29.607 --> 00:08:31.914
And because we do
kitchen every single day.

00:08:31.914 --> 00:08:34.044
No, we do chemistry every single day

00:08:34.044 --> 00:08:38.557
in outside of our kitchen
is heat, acid, bases.

00:08:40.354 --> 00:08:43.454
- He's now become known
as The Kitchen Chemist.

00:08:43.454 --> 00:08:46.278
Because what this teacher
did, he went above and beyond.

00:08:46.278 --> 00:08:49.301
He decided that if I'm going
to have lab experiments,

00:08:49.301 --> 00:08:52.264
my students need to have lab supplies.

00:08:52.264 --> 00:08:54.644
So taking money out of his own pocket,

00:08:54.644 --> 00:08:56.075
he went and bought the supplies

00:08:56.075 --> 00:08:58.083
and then delivered them personally

00:08:58.083 --> 00:09:00.914
to each one of his 35 students.

00:09:00.914 --> 00:09:04.484
And in so doing, he's
putting up these videos.

00:09:04.484 --> 00:09:06.504
These videos have gone viral.

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:09.234
Not only around the country,
but around the world.

00:09:09.234 --> 00:09:12.594
People are tuning in to Mr. Lee's classes.

00:09:12.594 --> 00:09:13.662
And I said to him,

00:09:13.662 --> 00:09:17.384
I didn't even think that
students would want to do that.

00:09:17.384 --> 00:09:19.644
He's got perfect attendance

00:09:19.644 --> 00:09:22.134
with his 35 students every single day.

00:09:22.134 --> 00:09:24.004
Once again, I think a testament

00:09:24.004 --> 00:09:27.044
to a teacher who cares
so much about his kids,

00:09:27.044 --> 00:09:29.754
that he said I'm gonna figure out a way

00:09:29.754 --> 00:09:33.124
to continue to connect with
them, even if it's remotely.

00:09:33.124 --> 00:09:35.374
And he has a good time
doing it. Lots of fun.

00:09:36.674 --> 00:09:38.534
- Well, I mean, that's
a fascinating story.

00:09:38.534 --> 00:09:41.188
And you know, my selfishly,
my Khan Academy hat,

00:09:41.188 --> 00:09:42.884
we're actually doing a big push

00:09:42.884 --> 00:09:44.964
in middle school and
high school chemistry.

00:09:44.964 --> 00:09:46.544
And I think you might've
identified someone

00:09:46.544 --> 00:09:48.523
that we have to call up
to help us out with that.

00:09:48.523 --> 00:09:52.094
- In fact, Sal, he's already connected.

00:09:52.094 --> 00:09:56.704
He goes to Khan Academy
for some of his advice

00:09:56.704 --> 00:09:57.814
and some suggestions

00:09:57.814 --> 00:09:59.854
and ideas that can help him with his kids.

00:09:59.854 --> 00:10:01.487
So you two already linked.

00:10:02.534 --> 00:10:04.294
- Well, yeah, I mean, it's
great if he can leverage us,

00:10:04.294 --> 00:10:05.624
but I think we might need to leverage him.

00:10:05.624 --> 00:10:10.364
So that's a separate
thread that we'll take on.

00:10:10.364 --> 00:10:12.798
So a ton of questions are coming out

00:10:12.798 --> 00:10:15.974
and a lot of it about, you know,

00:10:15.974 --> 00:10:17.774
your life and your life journey.

00:10:17.774 --> 00:10:19.844
So before I get some of these questions,

00:10:19.844 --> 00:10:22.834
I am always fascinated
by hearing the story.

00:10:22.834 --> 00:10:25.244
I think a lot of kids, if
when they're 10 years old

00:10:25.244 --> 00:10:28.634
or 15 years old, they say, I
want to become a journalist,

00:10:28.634 --> 00:10:31.567
or I want to be on NBC Nightly News.

00:10:32.674 --> 00:10:35.424
Your parents will be like,
all right, maybe, you know,

00:10:35.424 --> 00:10:36.994
your parents are kinda thinking about

00:10:36.994 --> 00:10:38.134
the probabilities in your head.

00:10:38.134 --> 00:10:39.694
And it's like, well, don't why
don't you just go, you know,

00:10:39.694 --> 00:10:41.924
do something that, you
know, go be an engineer,

00:10:41.924 --> 00:10:44.344
go be a doctor, a lawyer,
or something like that.

00:10:44.344 --> 00:10:47.856
School teacher, where
there's a clear pathway.

00:10:47.856 --> 00:10:51.384
Did you always know that you were gonna be

00:10:51.384 --> 00:10:53.514
a journalist, especially
a journalist on TV.

00:10:53.514 --> 00:10:55.414
And, how did you get to where you are?

00:10:56.274 --> 00:10:57.803
- I absolutely did not know

00:10:57.803 --> 00:11:00.844
that I was going to be
a broadcast journalist.

00:11:00.844 --> 00:11:05.674
It was not until my
sophomore year of college

00:11:05.674 --> 00:11:10.674
that I had a professor and
her name is Andrea Russian.

00:11:11.234 --> 00:11:13.884
And she looked at a paper that
I wrote about James Baldwin.

00:11:13.884 --> 00:11:17.614
And she said to me, I
was a sociology major.

00:11:17.614 --> 00:11:19.934
She said, "The conclusion you came to

00:11:19.934 --> 00:11:23.574
in the paper was not exactly
right, but the way you came

00:11:23.574 --> 00:11:25.778
to that conclusion and wrote
about it was brilliant."

00:11:25.778 --> 00:11:27.891
I respected this teacher so much,

00:11:27.891 --> 00:11:29.881
also an African-American woman,

00:11:29.881 --> 00:11:33.198
that it changed me at that
point, because she said

00:11:33.198 --> 00:11:36.124
I would encourage you to be a writer.

00:11:36.124 --> 00:11:37.344
I was just blown away.

00:11:37.344 --> 00:11:38.934
And I think it's one of those things

00:11:38.934 --> 00:11:41.090
about the real value and importance

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:44.394
and significance that a teacher
can have in someone's life.

00:11:44.394 --> 00:11:47.114
We've been profiling such a teacher,

00:11:47.114 --> 00:11:48.884
also on Nightly News this week.

00:11:48.884 --> 00:11:51.554
A young man who was
growing up in a shelter.

00:11:51.554 --> 00:11:53.844
And he had a fourth grade
teacher who kept telling him

00:11:53.844 --> 00:11:56.003
you can be anything that you want to be.

00:11:56.003 --> 00:11:59.099
He's now paying it forward.
And he's a fifth grade teacher.

00:11:59.099 --> 00:12:02.584
So teachers are outrageously important

00:12:02.584 --> 00:12:04.594
in a young person's life.

00:12:04.594 --> 00:12:07.074
So, in my sophomore year of college,

00:12:07.074 --> 00:12:10.364
I was inspired to take
some journalism courses.

00:12:10.364 --> 00:12:12.474
I did not know what I would do with them.

00:12:12.474 --> 00:12:14.654
But I took the journalism courses.

00:12:14.654 --> 00:12:18.328
I then had internships,
which are so valuable.

00:12:18.328 --> 00:12:22.344
I did, I had a college radio station.

00:12:22.344 --> 00:12:24.514
I was the host and the producer

00:12:24.514 --> 00:12:26.814
and the anchor of this
college radio station.

00:12:26.814 --> 00:12:28.904
I was doing public affairs programming.

00:12:28.904 --> 00:12:32.914
And then I also wrote
for my local newspaper.

00:12:32.914 --> 00:12:34.894
And then I went away.

00:12:34.894 --> 00:12:37.164
I went to East Africa
for a couple of years

00:12:37.164 --> 00:12:39.134
'cause I wasn't quite sure that journalism

00:12:39.134 --> 00:12:41.184
was the thing that I wanted to do.

00:12:41.184 --> 00:12:45.564
And I came back after I got
accepted to Columbia University

00:12:45.564 --> 00:12:48.164
Graduate School of Journalism.

00:12:48.164 --> 00:12:51.864
And that then launched
me into doing radio.

00:12:51.864 --> 00:12:53.454
And from radio I did television.

00:12:53.454 --> 00:12:57.669
And in television, I ended
up in New York here with NBC.

00:12:57.669 --> 00:13:02.669
Not what I had planned, but
I am enormously grateful

00:13:03.104 --> 00:13:05.624
for where my journey has taken me.

00:13:05.624 --> 00:13:07.967
And all because of a
teacher who inspired me.

00:13:09.184 --> 00:13:11.394
- You know, that has a lot
of parallels to Lester Holt.

00:13:11.394 --> 00:13:13.884
Lester Holt was on this show.

00:13:13.884 --> 00:13:15.854
I always feel embarrassed
calling people who

00:13:15.854 --> 00:13:17.637
do real shows, calling this a show.

00:13:18.994 --> 00:13:21.134
He was talking about being
kind of this radio geek

00:13:21.134 --> 00:13:22.204
even when he was very young.

00:13:22.204 --> 00:13:24.674
He would pretend to do a radio show,

00:13:24.674 --> 00:13:25.774
even when no one was listening.

00:13:25.774 --> 00:13:26.607
And eventually he did.

00:13:26.607 --> 00:13:29.370
So it sounds like radio is
a really interesting path.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.434
Before I get to some
other folks questions,

00:13:32.434 --> 00:13:35.594
what do you think allowed
you to really stand out?

00:13:35.594 --> 00:13:37.034
What are the skills that you think you,

00:13:37.034 --> 00:13:39.519
and I know it's an awkward
question to answer,

00:13:39.519 --> 00:13:42.474
but what are the things that
you think have really stood out

00:13:42.474 --> 00:13:44.474
and allowed you to get to where you are?

00:13:46.744 --> 00:13:48.644
- I actually think timing is everything.

00:13:48.644 --> 00:13:51.144
Timing is everything.

00:13:51.144 --> 00:13:55.744
The time that I had with
that professor in college.

00:13:55.744 --> 00:13:58.403
Having the time to have radio stations

00:13:58.403 --> 00:14:01.771
who's looked at me and said,
"We will let you do this."

00:14:03.154 --> 00:14:05.984
Then getting accepted to a great school.

00:14:05.984 --> 00:14:08.464
And from that great school
I got a scholarship.

00:14:08.464 --> 00:14:11.534
And the scholarship, it
was with what was called

00:14:11.534 --> 00:14:13.684
Westinghouse Broadcasting back then.

00:14:13.684 --> 00:14:17.604
And they wanted to, I think,
cash in on their investment.

00:14:17.604 --> 00:14:19.304
They gave me some money
and they wanted to see

00:14:19.304 --> 00:14:20.694
if they get follow through on it.

00:14:20.694 --> 00:14:24.664
And I ended up in Pittsburgh,
Pennsylvania doing radio

00:14:24.664 --> 00:14:25.754
and then to television.

00:14:25.754 --> 00:14:30.754
I had a fantastic mentor
who saw something in me

00:14:31.344 --> 00:14:36.263
and encouraged me to do
things that I quite honestly,

00:14:36.263 --> 00:14:39.814
unlike Lester, in imagining
myself doing radio.

00:14:39.814 --> 00:14:41.847
I hadn't imagined myself doing it.

00:14:43.204 --> 00:14:47.674
But, having an opportunity
to write and in radio.

00:14:47.674 --> 00:14:49.404
I loved doing radio.

00:14:49.404 --> 00:14:52.824
One of the strengths of it
is, you have to create images

00:14:52.824 --> 00:14:53.657
with your words.

00:14:53.657 --> 00:14:56.594
People can't see, but they can hear.

00:14:56.594 --> 00:14:59.464
So all of the sounds,
the sound of my voice,

00:14:59.464 --> 00:15:02.284
the sound of the voices of
the people whom I interviewed,

00:15:02.284 --> 00:15:05.124
the sounds in the
neighborhood or the streets

00:15:05.124 --> 00:15:06.954
where the places where I went.

00:15:06.954 --> 00:15:09.254
Using all of those
sounds to craft a story.

00:15:09.254 --> 00:15:11.204
It was fascinating to me.

00:15:11.204 --> 00:15:14.154
And I, again, I think some of it is luck.

00:15:14.154 --> 00:15:16.384
Some of it is being in the
right place at the right time.

00:15:16.384 --> 00:15:20.534
Some of it is having people
who encourage you to go on

00:15:20.534 --> 00:15:23.254
and do things that sometimes
you don't even imagine

00:15:23.254 --> 00:15:24.684
that you can do.

00:15:24.684 --> 00:15:29.684
And then also, I must say
having the parents that I had.

00:15:30.894 --> 00:15:34.327
Who just always told me
that once I put my mind

00:15:34.327 --> 00:15:37.074
to something, I could do it.

00:15:37.074 --> 00:15:38.934
And so I've always followed that.

00:15:38.934 --> 00:15:40.587
It just keeps me going forward.

00:15:41.764 --> 00:15:44.224
- Were there any moments
where you've kind of

00:15:44.224 --> 00:15:46.560
doubted yourself, where you felt like,

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:47.884
hey maybe I'm not cut out for this,

00:15:47.884 --> 00:15:49.834
or I'm not gonna be able to make the dent

00:15:49.834 --> 00:15:50.787
that I wanna have?

00:15:51.784 --> 00:15:53.644
(Rehema sighing)

00:15:53.644 --> 00:15:54.574
- Not really.

00:15:54.574 --> 00:15:58.044
But the moment that I felt
when I was first starting

00:15:58.044 --> 00:16:01.964
in the business, is that all
of the monumental stories

00:16:01.964 --> 00:16:03.374
had already been told.

00:16:03.374 --> 00:16:04.484
Vietnam had happened.

00:16:04.484 --> 00:16:06.434
The Civil Rights Movement had happened.

00:16:08.424 --> 00:16:10.516
Pearl Harbor had happened.

00:16:10.516 --> 00:16:12.854
So what was I going to do?

00:16:12.854 --> 00:16:15.404
A kid coming up in the eighties,
going into the business

00:16:15.404 --> 00:16:19.454
And I thought my life
was pretty good actually.

00:16:19.454 --> 00:16:22.044
And the world around me was opening up.

00:16:22.044 --> 00:16:25.044
And opportunities for
people who looked like me

00:16:25.044 --> 00:16:26.324
were beginning to open up.

00:16:26.324 --> 00:16:28.034
And so what would the stories be

00:16:28.034 --> 00:16:29.527
that I could possibly tell.

00:16:30.444 --> 00:16:31.734
Boy was I wrong.

00:16:31.734 --> 00:16:34.644
And there has just been
one story after another.

00:16:34.644 --> 00:16:38.283
And here we are now, in the
middle of a historic moment

00:16:38.283 --> 00:16:40.369
in terms of a pandemic.

00:16:40.369 --> 00:16:41.854
I couldn't have imagined it.

00:16:41.854 --> 00:16:44.244
And I don't think that anybody
else could have either.

00:16:44.244 --> 00:16:46.460
But the minute you think that there's not

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:50.684
a monumental story to tell, life changes.

00:16:50.684 --> 00:16:52.027
And it can be your story.

00:16:53.444 --> 00:16:54.844
- Absolutely.

00:16:54.844 --> 00:16:56.984
You know, there's a couple
of questions here about,

00:16:56.984 --> 00:16:58.994
I'll ask both of them and
we can kind of merge them.

00:16:58.994 --> 00:17:01.091
From YouTube, Smart Bear says,

00:17:01.091 --> 00:17:03.344
"How do you include your
NBC life with your kids?"

00:17:03.344 --> 00:17:05.654
I'm assuming they're asking
about kind of work-life balance.

00:17:05.654 --> 00:17:08.901
And also from YouTube Niche786 is asking,

00:17:08.901 --> 00:17:11.084
"What are some things that
you notice now in your life

00:17:11.084 --> 00:17:13.294
after becoming a journalist
that didn't notice before?"

00:17:13.294 --> 00:17:15.025
So generally speaking, you know,

00:17:15.025 --> 00:17:17.384
life work balance is a
struggle for everyone.

00:17:17.384 --> 00:17:19.154
I can imagine you travel a lot.

00:17:19.154 --> 00:17:19.987
You're on the road a lot.

00:17:19.987 --> 00:17:21.544
How do you make these balances

00:17:21.544 --> 00:17:23.244
and how has it affected your life?

00:17:24.114 --> 00:17:26.514
- One of the things that
happened when my child,

00:17:26.514 --> 00:17:29.407
my son came home was,

00:17:31.124 --> 00:17:32.534
to be honest with you,

00:17:32.534 --> 00:17:35.284
it was a rebirth for me.

00:17:35.284 --> 00:17:39.164
I began to see life completely differently

00:17:39.164 --> 00:17:41.254
from how I'd seen it before.

00:17:41.254 --> 00:17:44.754
And the things that worried
me didn't worry me anymore.

00:17:44.754 --> 00:17:48.804
I was so consumed with
trying to make certain

00:17:48.804 --> 00:17:51.251
that I gave my child
the best possible life.

00:17:51.251 --> 00:17:54.004
And at the same time, I'm a single parent.

00:17:54.004 --> 00:17:57.594
And so I had to keep working.

00:17:57.594 --> 00:18:01.504
and I am so fortunate that
I've had wonderful people

00:18:01.504 --> 00:18:04.944
in my life to work with me and to help me.

00:18:04.944 --> 00:18:08.964
So it's possible that if the phone rings

00:18:08.964 --> 00:18:12.254
I can get up and go to that destination

00:18:12.254 --> 00:18:14.047
where the story is unfolding.

00:18:15.149 --> 00:18:17.544
And that's about luck.

00:18:17.544 --> 00:18:21.314
And I think it's also
about positioning yourself

00:18:21.314 --> 00:18:24.724
in your life and asking, quite
honestly, to reveal to you,

00:18:24.724 --> 00:18:28.054
to ask the universe,
to help me do the kinds

00:18:28.054 --> 00:18:30.859
of things that I want to
do in order to balance

00:18:30.859 --> 00:18:33.414
life with my work.

00:18:33.414 --> 00:18:36.604
My work is enormously important to me.

00:18:36.604 --> 00:18:38.444
I can't imagine doing anything else.

00:18:38.444 --> 00:18:41.107
I really, I really love this job.

00:18:42.174 --> 00:18:44.834
And I love my family.

00:18:44.834 --> 00:18:47.424
And people say, "Can you have it all?"

00:18:47.424 --> 00:18:50.664
Sometimes you can. Sometimes you can't.

00:18:50.664 --> 00:18:54.094
Sometimes your child needs
to go to the pediatrician.

00:18:54.094 --> 00:18:55.484
And a story is breaking.

00:18:55.484 --> 00:18:57.854
But your child's needs to
go to the pediatrician.

00:18:57.854 --> 00:18:59.794
Story. Pediatrician.

00:18:59.794 --> 00:19:02.034
It really isn't that much of a hard call

00:19:02.034 --> 00:19:04.744
to make if your child needs something.

00:19:04.744 --> 00:19:08.334
And I again have been so fortunate.

00:19:08.334 --> 00:19:11.657
I work for a company that also recognizes

00:19:11.657 --> 00:19:15.884
that I am a correspondent,
but I'm also a human being.

00:19:15.884 --> 00:19:19.844
And I think that NBC appreciates the fact

00:19:19.844 --> 00:19:24.844
that I will give my all every
single moment that I can.

00:19:24.954 --> 00:19:28.415
And when it's time to
take time for my family

00:19:28.415 --> 00:19:30.154
I have to do that too.

00:19:30.154 --> 00:19:32.884
That makes me a better journalist.

00:19:32.884 --> 00:19:35.440
I think it makes all of
us better at what we do

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:37.342
when we can have a little compassion

00:19:37.342 --> 00:19:42.342
and understanding about a
little one who is dependent

00:19:42.846 --> 00:19:45.334
on us in order to grow.

00:19:45.334 --> 00:19:48.604
So to that first question
about how do I balance it.

00:19:48.604 --> 00:19:50.447
I balance it the best way I can.

00:19:50.447 --> 00:19:53.987
But I also have help to make
it possible for me to do it.

00:19:55.384 --> 00:19:56.804
- No, it's a powerful idea because I think

00:19:56.804 --> 00:19:58.134
a lot of times when
people talk about this,

00:19:58.134 --> 00:20:00.914
they talk about, oh you should balance,

00:20:00.914 --> 00:20:01.854
like it's a trade off.

00:20:01.854 --> 00:20:03.861
That, hey, you're gonna
take away from work

00:20:03.861 --> 00:20:05.724
to become a better parent.

00:20:05.724 --> 00:20:08.074
But to your point, especially
when you're reporting

00:20:08.074 --> 00:20:11.544
about humanity, that element
that's going to make you

00:20:11.544 --> 00:20:14.204
a better reporter to have that,
and a better correspondent

00:20:14.204 --> 00:20:15.924
to have that aspect of your life.

00:20:15.924 --> 00:20:17.904
I couldn't, I couldn't
agree with that more.

00:20:17.904 --> 00:20:19.854
You know, we have a
limited amount of time.

00:20:19.854 --> 00:20:22.714
One thing that I, questions, actually,

00:20:22.714 --> 00:20:24.447
there's a lot of questions coming in.

00:20:27.423 --> 00:20:29.204
I'll save one of these for the end.

00:20:29.204 --> 00:20:32.754
But I am curious your views on just

00:20:32.754 --> 00:20:37.754
the state of eighties and
nineties, when you know

00:20:38.504 --> 00:20:40.544
the Nightly News was everything.

00:20:40.544 --> 00:20:42.744
Now we talk about social media.

00:20:42.744 --> 00:20:44.963
Oh, it sounds like I might've clipped off.

00:20:44.963 --> 00:20:49.284
I would love your take on where
we are journalism generally.

00:20:49.284 --> 00:20:51.234
You know, there's a lot of
talk about polarization.

00:20:51.234 --> 00:20:52.684
People living in their bubbles.

00:20:52.684 --> 00:20:54.884
Going on social media,
getting their own version.

00:20:54.884 --> 00:20:57.649
At the same time, we still
have the Nightly News,

00:20:57.649 --> 00:21:00.392
which is kind of the, you
know, which we've had,

00:21:00.392 --> 00:21:02.744
the three major networks, you know,

00:21:02.744 --> 00:21:04.844
since for many, many decades.

00:21:04.844 --> 00:21:06.344
How do you see all of this evolving?

00:21:06.344 --> 00:21:07.704
And how has it affected, you know

00:21:07.704 --> 00:21:09.354
how y'all think about journalism?

00:21:10.344 --> 00:21:11.704
- The way I think about it is that

00:21:11.704 --> 00:21:14.654
this is we're in this
little bubble of infancy

00:21:14.654 --> 00:21:18.084
of communication and information.

00:21:18.084 --> 00:21:19.744
Technology is exploding.

00:21:19.744 --> 00:21:23.314
Information is coming at us
fast and furious and constant.

00:21:23.314 --> 00:21:26.204
And I often think of it,
and I don't mean this

00:21:26.204 --> 00:21:28.404
to be disparaging in any way whatsoever,

00:21:28.404 --> 00:21:31.784
so please forgive me if someone
attempts to see it that way.

00:21:31.784 --> 00:21:34.824
I often think of it as us being very young

00:21:34.824 --> 00:21:37.684
and almost childlike in the fact that

00:21:37.684 --> 00:21:40.184
we are bombarded with information.

00:21:40.184 --> 00:21:43.074
It's almost like a child in
a candy store who is hungry

00:21:43.074 --> 00:21:46.054
for every single bit
of candy and sweetness

00:21:46.054 --> 00:21:47.004
that they can find.

00:21:47.004 --> 00:21:48.844
And it's all around them.

00:21:48.844 --> 00:21:53.194
And ultimately the child learns
that there has to be a diet

00:21:53.194 --> 00:21:55.774
of candy and other things.

00:21:55.774 --> 00:22:00.534
Protein, other carbohydrates
and vegetables and vitamins.

00:22:00.534 --> 00:22:01.624
You learned that.

00:22:01.624 --> 00:22:04.273
But at the beginning, it's just
an explosion of excitement.

00:22:04.273 --> 00:22:06.484
I think we're in that
explosion of excitement.

00:22:06.484 --> 00:22:08.484
There's information everywhere.

00:22:08.484 --> 00:22:09.724
And I'm in the information business.

00:22:09.724 --> 00:22:11.324
So I would never be one to say

00:22:11.324 --> 00:22:12.825
let's turn off the information.

00:22:12.825 --> 00:22:14.894
But one of the things
I do think is important

00:22:14.894 --> 00:22:18.134
is to understand and to get
a sense of media literacy.

00:22:18.134 --> 00:22:19.684
To know the difference between something

00:22:19.684 --> 00:22:21.454
that's just exploding at you,

00:22:21.454 --> 00:22:25.144
and something that gives
you context to the content.

00:22:25.144 --> 00:22:29.084
And I want all of us to
become really, really literate

00:22:29.084 --> 00:22:30.814
and understanding media.

00:22:30.814 --> 00:22:33.134
Question sources. Examine sources.

00:22:33.134 --> 00:22:35.281
And maybe go outside of our comfort zone

00:22:35.281 --> 00:22:38.044
and look at a variety of different things.

00:22:38.044 --> 00:22:41.194
In so doing, I think we'll
become better educated

00:22:41.194 --> 00:22:45.375
on how to use this wonderful,
wonderful opportunity

00:22:45.375 --> 00:22:48.074
of information that we have available

00:22:48.074 --> 00:22:50.197
to us right at our fingertips.

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:53.684
- It makes a lot of sense.

00:22:53.684 --> 00:22:55.374
And maybe just to close out, you know

00:22:55.374 --> 00:22:58.124
one last question getting
from YouTube again.

00:22:58.124 --> 00:23:00.264
Actually another question from Niche786.

00:23:00.264 --> 00:23:03.214
What some advice that you
would give to teenagers?

00:23:03.214 --> 00:23:05.705
I guess some teenagers
who might want to go

00:23:05.705 --> 00:23:06.694
down a path similar to
yours or people want to go

00:23:06.694 --> 00:23:07.527
down any path.

00:23:07.527 --> 00:23:09.684
What advice would you
give to your teenage self

00:23:09.684 --> 00:23:11.824
or to any teenage self?

00:23:11.824 --> 00:23:16.014
- Read everything you can.
Information really is power.

00:23:16.014 --> 00:23:20.254
It also informs your thinking.
It informs your writing.

00:23:20.254 --> 00:23:22.034
It informs your perspective.

00:23:22.034 --> 00:23:23.734
So you have to read.

00:23:23.734 --> 00:23:26.184
Sometimes we get just little tweets

00:23:26.184 --> 00:23:28.254
and think that now I'm really informed.

00:23:28.254 --> 00:23:31.654
Well, you might be really excited.

00:23:31.654 --> 00:23:36.380
And maybe excited to go examine
something more in depth.

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:38.174
One of the things that happens

00:23:38.174 --> 00:23:40.674
and was said to me when I
started in the business is that,

00:23:40.674 --> 00:23:44.334
journalists, we're kind of
in a 30 minute broadcast

00:23:44.334 --> 00:23:45.800
of nightly news.

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:47.054
We're a conduit of information.

00:23:47.054 --> 00:23:50.284
We certainly do frame
what people think about.

00:23:50.284 --> 00:23:53.191
But I hope that we
encourage people to say,

00:23:53.191 --> 00:23:58.191
"Now let me decide what I
think about the information

00:23:58.284 --> 00:24:02.214
that you have channeled
for me to take a look at."

00:24:02.214 --> 00:24:04.084
And the only way we can do that, you know,

00:24:04.084 --> 00:24:07.761
when you get a story, I hope
that people then will say,

00:24:07.761 --> 00:24:09.694
"Let me read more about this.

00:24:09.694 --> 00:24:12.784
Let me find out more about
that particular subject.

00:24:12.784 --> 00:24:15.484
Even let me find more about these teachers

00:24:15.484 --> 00:24:18.754
who are inspiring all of us
and how we might be able to

00:24:18.754 --> 00:24:21.044
appreciate them a little bit more."

00:24:21.044 --> 00:24:25.354
Not just on a short news
clip that we provide,

00:24:25.354 --> 00:24:28.994
but going more, exploring more.

00:24:28.994 --> 00:24:31.144
I hope that anyone who
thinks about getting

00:24:31.144 --> 00:24:33.784
in this business will become a glutton

00:24:33.784 --> 00:24:36.984
for information and all
kinds of information.

00:24:36.984 --> 00:24:40.924
That's what makes what we
do powerful and important

00:24:40.924 --> 00:24:43.417
to the people who are our
viewers or our readers.

00:24:44.854 --> 00:24:46.134
- It makes a ton of sense.

00:24:46.134 --> 00:24:48.434
Rehema, I want to thank you for joining

00:24:48.434 --> 00:24:49.704
and I actually want to congratulate you.

00:24:49.704 --> 00:24:52.074
You're getting a major
award, the Legacy Award,

00:24:52.074 --> 00:24:53.705
from the National Association
of Black Journalists.

00:24:53.705 --> 00:24:55.664
That's already been announced.

00:24:55.664 --> 00:24:57.914
But you're going to get the
award I think in a few days.

00:24:57.914 --> 00:24:59.894
But this is just another testament

00:24:59.894 --> 00:25:03.664
to the importance of the
work and your career.

00:25:03.664 --> 00:25:06.104
And thank you for being part of this

00:25:06.104 --> 00:25:08.254
and giving us a little bit of wisdom.

00:25:08.254 --> 00:25:10.254
- It's a pleasure. Thanks for having me.

00:25:11.334 --> 00:25:12.167
- Thank you.

00:25:13.504 --> 00:25:15.434
Well, everyone. Thanks for joining.

00:25:15.434 --> 00:25:17.264
You know these conversations
always go faster

00:25:17.264 --> 00:25:18.154
than I expected.

00:25:18.154 --> 00:25:20.944
I could have talked to Rehema
for a very, very long time.

00:25:20.944 --> 00:25:23.344
But definitely check out NBC Nightly News

00:25:23.344 --> 00:25:25.734
and these stories that
really are incredible stories

00:25:25.734 --> 00:25:27.924
on education and teachers.

00:25:27.924 --> 00:25:29.824
And you know, for those of you all who are

00:25:29.824 --> 00:25:30.794
gonna be future journalists,

00:25:30.794 --> 00:25:32.974
I think you just heard a great role model.

00:25:32.974 --> 00:25:35.054
To think about how you
might chart your career.

00:25:35.054 --> 00:25:38.004
So anyway, I will see you at the next

00:25:38.004 --> 00:25:40.304
Homeroom With Sal.

00:25:40.304 --> 00:25:44.354
It's gonna be a Ed Talk
tomorrow with Brooke Mabry.

00:25:44.354 --> 00:25:45.837
I will talk to you all then.

